{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:uniform-coverage",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For the donor-only interval \\(I_n^{\\mathrm{out}}\\), \\(\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal P_n}P\\{\\theta_n\\in I_n^{\\mathrm{out}}\\}\\ge1-\\alpha\\), with no lower bound on either target loading.  Under the canonical embedding in \\(\\textup{prop:yan-wainwright-class-embedding}\\), this extends the coverage guarantee of the loading-restricted Yan--Wainwright Gaussian theorem on the embedded square one-target subclass: it includes joint-zero and arbitrarily slow loading transitions that violate condition \\(4.4\\mathrm{c}\\).  It makes no claim of an extension over the full published Yan--Wainwright class.",
    "depends_on": [
      "def:analytic-interval",
      "lem:conditional-reduction",
      "lem:gaussian-outer-inversion",
      "lem:yan-wainwright-gaussian-condition",
      "prop:yan-wainwright-class-embedding"
    ],
    "justification": "Uniform coverage across the joint-zero and slow-transition cone is the central inferential claim.",
    "gap": "Yan--Wainwright's Gaussian theorem assumes condition \\(4.4\\mathrm c\\); after correcting their noise normalization, the canonical embedded subclass still satisfies conditions \\(4.1\\)--\\(4.4\\mathrm b\\), while joint-zero loadings violate condition \\(4.4\\mathrm c\\).",
    "consumer": "CAST users obtain valid missing-mean uncertainty even when fitted target factor loadings are nearly zero, within the stated strong-donor subclass.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P=P_{A,u,v}\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  The definition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) gives \\(\\|u\\|_2\\le B\\sqrt n\\), \\(\\|v\\|_2\\le B\\sqrt n\\), and \\(\\|A^+\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=s_r(A)^{-1}\\le(cn)^{-1}\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n|\\theta_n|=|uA^+v|\n\\le\\|u\\|_2\\|A^+\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\|v\\|_2\n\\le\\frac{B^2}{c}=M_0.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThus the off-gate interval \\([-M_0,M_0]\\) always covers \\(\\theta_n\\).\n\nOn \\(G_n\\), condition on \\(X\\).  By \\(\\textup{lem:conditional-reduction}\\),\n\\[\nZ_n=m_n+\\xi_n,\\qquad \\xi_n\\mid X\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_{2r}),\n\\qquad\n\\theta_{X,n}=\\sigma^2m_{p,n}^{\\mathsf T}D_nm_{q,n},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal P_n}\nP\\bigl(|\\theta_{X,n}-\\theta_n|>d_n,\\,G_n\\bigr)\n\\le e^{-n/2}+3(e+n)^{-6}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe gate gives \\(s_r(X)>0\\), so every diagonal entry of \\(D_n=\\widehat S^{-1}\\) is positive.  Applying \\(\\textup{lem:gaussian-outer-inversion}\\) conditionally at this realized \\(D_n\\) and \\(m_n\\) yields\n\\[\nP\\left(\n|\\widehat\\theta_n-\\theta_{X,n}|>\\sigma^2W_n\n\\,\\middle|\\,X\\right)\\le\\alpha\n\\quad\\hbox{on \\(G_n\\)}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nIf neither failure in (3) and (4) occurs, the triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n|\\widehat\\theta_n-\\theta_n|\n\\le\\sigma^2W_n+d_n,\n\\]\nwhich is precisely on-gate membership of \\(\\theta_n\\) in \\(I_n^{\\mathrm{out}}\\).  Combining this fact with (1), integrating (4) over \\(X\\), and using (3),\n\\[\n\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal P_n}P\\{\\theta_n\\notin I_n^{\\mathrm{out}}\\}\n\\le\\alpha+e^{-n/2}+3(e+n)^{-6}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nTaking the limit superior proves the asserted uniform coverage.  No step divides by \\(u\\), \\(v\\), or a loading norm.\n\nFor the comparison clause, \\(\\textup{prop:yan-wainwright-class-embedding}\\) identifies \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with a canonical square one-target subclass satisfying Yan--Wainwright conditions \\(4.1\\)--\\(4.4\\mathrm b\\) for sufficiently large \\(n\\).  The cited node \\(\\textup{lem:yan-wainwright-gaussian-condition}\\) records that their Theorem~1 additionally assumes condition \\(4.4\\mathrm c\\).  At \\(u=v=0\\), the law remains in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), (5) still applies, and the embedding proposition proves that condition \\(4.4\\mathrm c\\) fails.  Hence the new coverage result includes joint-zero and arbitrarily slow loading transitions outside that loading-restricted theorem on the embedded subclass.  This argument neither identifies \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with, nor claims an extension over, the full published class.",
